# Component Library Architecture

Atlas Design System monorepo. **CSS Modules only** — no Tailwind.

## Stack

- React 19, TypeScript, Biome (2-space indent)
- Turborepo workspaces: `apps/*`, `packages/*`
- Colors: `@spiffdog-design/atlas-colors` (OKLab palette scales with `light-dark()`)
- Components: `@spiffdog-design/atlas-components`
- Utilities: `@spiffdog-design/atlas-tools`
- Docs: Storybook in `apps/docs` (stories colocated in `packages/atlas-components/src` and `packages/atlas-colors/src/preview`)
- Headless UI (when native HTML/CSS is insufficient): `@base-ui/react` — behavior/a11y only; never reimplement dialogs, menus, focus traps
- Tests: Vitest + Testing Library; Storybook for all shared components

```ts
import { Button } from "@spiffdog-design/atlas-components";
import { palette } from "@spiffdog-design/atlas-colors";
import "@spiffdog-design/atlas-components/styles.css";
```

Storybook resolves package imports via Vite aliases in `apps/docs/.storybook/main.ts`.

## Monorepo layout

```
apps/docs/                       # Storybook — MDX docs + preview config
packages/
  atlas-colors/                  # Palette tokens (TS + CSS custom properties)
  atlas-components/              # Shared React components
    src/
      styles/                    # index.css, tokens.css, globals.css, component.module.template.css
      button/                    # Per-component folder (organize by type as library grows)
      index.ts
  atlas-tools/                   # Global utilities and helpers
    src/
  biome-config/
  typescript-config/
```

Per component (e.g. `packages/atlas-components/src/button/`):

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `index.tsx` | React component — JSX, hooks, handlers that delegate to utils |
| `button.module.css` | Layered CSS Module styles |
| `button.stories.tsx` | Storybook (`atlas components/Button`) |
| `button.utils.ts` | Pure functions — validation, formatters, type guards, defaults |
| `button.test.ts` | Vitest unit tests for **every export** in `button.utils.ts` |

## Styling rules

1. **CSS cascade layers** — declared in `packages/atlas-components/src/styles/index.css`:
   ```css
   @layer theme, components, variants, vendor;
   ```
   | Layer | Contents | Priority |
   |-------|----------|----------|
   | `theme` | atlas-colors, `tokens.css`, `globals.css` | lowest |
   | `components` | shared component structure in `*.module.css` | |
   | `variants` | `data-*` props and headless UI state in `*.module.css` | |
   | `vendor` | third-party library overrides | |
   | *(unlayered)* | consumer app `*.module.css`, ad-hoc UI tweaks | **highest** |

   Shared components: structure in `@layer components`, variants/state in `@layer variants` (see `styles/component.module.template.css`). Consumer app modules stay **unlayered**.

2. **CSS Modules** for all component styles. Global CSS only via layered imports in `styles/index.css`. Storybook uses `localsConvention: "camelCaseOnly"`.
3. **Data-attributes for variants** — map props to `data-*` on the DOM; style in `@layer variants` (e.g. `[data-variant="primary"]`). No prop-driven conditional class names in JS.
4. **Headless UI state** — style via `[data-disabled]`, `[data-checked]`, `[data-highlighted]` in `@layer variants`, not `className` callbacks.
5. **Static classes only in JS** — join `styles.root` and passthrough `className`; no variant toggles in JS.
6. **Use tokens** from `styles/tokens.css` for spacing, sizing, color, radius, and typography.

## Platform-first behavior

**Prefer native HTML and CSS** for interaction, state, and accessibility. JavaScript is **progressive enhancement** — not the primary way to drive component behavior.

**Goal:** encode behavior in markup and styles so it works across frameworks (vanilla JS, Lit, Web Components, React, Angular, etc.) without reimplementing logic in each.

### Priority order

1. **Semantic HTML** — use the element that already does the job (`button`, `a`, `input`, `select`, `textarea`, `details`/`summary`, `dialog`, `fieldset`, `label`, etc.)
2. **CSS** — pseudo-classes (`:hover`, `:focus-visible`, `:checked`, `:disabled`), selectors (`:has()`), attribute selectors (`[data-*]`, `[aria-*]`), and **modern platform CSS** (see below) for layout, motion, and visual state
3. **Declarative DOM** — variants and state as **`data-*` and native attributes** on the element; consumers set attributes, CSS handles presentation
4. **JavaScript** — only when platform primitives are insufficient (complex keyboard models, focus traps, virtualization, async validation, etc.); delegate a11y-heavy patterns to `@base-ui/react` rather than bespoke handlers

### Prefer native primitives

| Need | Reach for | Not first |
|------|-----------|-----------|
| Disclosure / accordion | `details`/`summary`, `hidden` | `useState` + click toggles |
| Modal / alert | `<dialog>`, `form[method="dialog"]` | Portal + manual focus trap |
| Popover / tooltip (when supported) | Popover API + CSS anchor positioning, `title` | Positioning JS on `mouseenter` |
| Aligned overlays / menus | `anchor-name`, `position-anchor`, `position-area` | `getBoundingClientRect` + manual placement |
| Nested grid alignment | `subgrid` | JS-measured column sync |
| Form validation | `required`, `pattern`, `type`, `:invalid` | Custom validators before native fails |
| Toggle / selection | `checkbox`, `radio`, `select`, `:checked` | `onClick` + boolean state for styling |
| Navigation | `<a href>`, `<button type="button">` | `div` + `onClick` |

### Modern CSS platform features

**Prefer built-in CSS** over bespoke JS when evergreen browsers ship the behavior. Atlas targets current evergreen browsers; use these features directly rather than polyfilling or reimplementing in script.

Reach for platform CSS before JS equivalents:

| Capability | Prefer | Over |
|------------|--------|------|
| Anchor-linked positioning | `anchor-name`, `position-anchor`, `position-area`, `anchor-default` | Manual `top`/`left` from `getBoundingClientRect` |
| Nested grid alignment | `grid-template-columns: subgrid` (and rows) | Measuring children to sync tracks |
| Responsive component layout | `@container` / container query units (`cqw`, `cqi`) | ResizeObserver + breakpoint state |
| Entry / exit motion | `@starting-style`, `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` | Mount/unmount animation libraries |
| Scroll-linked effects | `animation-timeline: scroll()`, `scroll()` | Scroll listeners updating styles |
| Page / shared-element motion | `view-transition-name`, View Transitions API | FLIP layout JS |
| Intrinsic control sizing | `field-sizing: content` | Auto-resize textarea scripts |
| Color & theme mixing | `color-mix()`, `light-dark()` | Hardcoded light/dark duplicates |

This list is illustrative — adopt other **standard, evergreen** CSS/HTML platform features the same way. When support is uneven, layer a minimal fallback (simpler layout, reduced motion) rather than blocking on a JS shim.

### Component wrappers

React components should be **thin adapters**: render correct semantic markup, map props to `data-*`/native attributes, and add JS only where the platform falls short. When JS is required, keep it framework-agnostic in `{name}.utils.ts` so the same rules apply in other runtimes.

## Colors & tokens

**Palette** (`@spiffdog-design/atlas-colors`) — raw scales as `--{palette}{step}` (e.g. `--gray9`, `--blue8`). See `packages/atlas-colors/src/palette.ts` or Storybook **atlas colors** stories.

```css
@import "@spiffdog-design/atlas-colors/index.css";
```

```ts
import { palette } from "@spiffdog-design/atlas-colors";
const primary = palette.blue[9];
```

**Semantic tokens** (`styles/tokens.css`) — map palette to intent (`--color-bg`, `--color-text`, `--color-primary`, etc.). Components use `--color-*`, not raw palette steps.

**Theming**: atlas-colors sets `color-scheme: light dark` on `:root`. Force appearance via `document.documentElement.style.colorScheme`.

**Rem scale**: `html { font-size: 62.5%; }` in `globals.css` → **1rem = 10px**.

| Token | rem | px |
|-------|-----|-----|
| `--space-0-5` | 0.125rem | 1.25px |
| `--space-1` | 0.25rem | 2.5px |
| `--space-2` | 0.5rem | 5px |
| `--space-3` | 0.75rem | 7.5px |
| `--space-4` | 1rem | 10px |
| `--space-5` | 1.25rem | 12.5px |
| `--space-6` | 1.5rem | 15px |
| `--space-8` | 2rem | 20px |

Token categories in `tokens.css`: `--space-*`, `--control-height-*`, `--font-size-*`, `--radius-*`, `--color-*`, `--focus-ring`.

- Padding/margin/gap → `--space-*`; colors → `--color-*`; radius → `--radius-*`
- **OK to hardcode**: `1px` borders, `2px` outline width, `999px` pill radius, `0`/`100%`/`fit-content`, `@layer vendor` overrides
- **Do not** duplicate token values as literals — add to `tokens.css` when reused or design-intent

## Storybook

- `npm run dev` from repo root or `apps/docs`
- Story titles: `atlas components/{Name}`, `atlas colors/{Name}`
- Preview imports `@spiffdog-design/atlas-components/styles/index.css`

## Pure logic & testing

Extract anything testable without React or the DOM into `{name}.utils.ts`:

- Validation, defaults, type guards, formatters, mappers, computed constants

Keep in `index.tsx`: JSX, hooks, component state, and thin handlers that call utils.

**Unit tests are required.** Add `{name}.test.ts` (Vitest) covering every export from `{name}.utils.ts`. Do not skip because logic "looks small" — extract first, then test.

```typescript
// button.utils.ts
export function getButtonLabel(name: string) { return `Hello from ${name}`; }

// button.test.ts
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { getButtonLabel } from "./button.utils.js";

describe("getButtonLabel", () => {
  it("includes the app name", () => {
    expect(getButtonLabel("Atlas")).toBe("Hello from Atlas");
  });
});
```

Also required for every shared component: `*.stories.tsx`. Optional: `*.test.tsx` for render/a11y tests with Testing Library.

## Do not

- Add Tailwind or utility-first CSS frameworks
- Put variant styles in global CSS or inline styles
- Hardcode spacing/sizing/color/radius when a token exists
- Skip stories or `{name}.test.ts` for new shared components
- Inline testable logic in `index.tsx` instead of `{name}.utils.ts`
- Use `@mui/base` (deprecated) — use `@base-ui/react`
- Reimplement in JS what native HTML/CSS already provides (e.g. visibility toggles, basic disclosure, form validity styling, overlay positioning, subgrid alignment)
- Put primary interaction or visual state only in React handlers/state when equivalent `data-*`, attributes, or pseudo-classes would work
- Use non-semantic elements (`div`, `span`) as interactive controls when a native element exists
